unixlib-thread-ticker.patch: the thread ticker of UnixLib, after Andrew Youll's riscos-unixlib (5.0.3.3).

Credit: this is Andrew Youll's work (https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-unixlib, "an unofficial fork of GCCSDK's UnixLib"; the files pthread/ticker.c,
incl-local/internal/ticker.s, module/pthticker.s and the changes to pthread/_context.s, asm_dec.s, pthread.h, pthinit.c, context.c, sys/_syslib.s, sys/exec.c and vscript
are his, taken from his commits 8c5c824 to 8dae5f2 of September and October 2026, and carry his "2026" comments).  UnixLib is LGPL; so are these files.
What it fixes: UnixLib starts a system-wide OS_CallEvery ticker when a program has a second thread, and the handler ran from the program's own memory.  The ticker fires
whichever task is paged in: a program linked statically with libunixlib.a (jpegview, reported by Chris) made every other task abort with "abort on data transfer" or
"abort on instruction fetch" and then locked the machine (reproduced on a Raspberry Pi with tests/tickbug: a thread made BEFORE Wimp_Initialise starts the ticker with no
Wimp filters).  Now the ticker routines run from the RMA block (a copy made at start-up) or from the optional PThreadTicker module, the filters follow the task handle,
and a fork or exec leaves the parent's ticker alone.  The module (module/pthticker.s) is built by build-pthticker.sh.
Adapted to our tree: Makefile.in (the generated one) gets the ticker.c rules; __pthread_prog_fini keeps our "image shared with a vfork child" return after his ticker check.

